DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  legacy  function ttttyysslloott() returns the index of the current user’s
       entry in some file.

       Now "What file?" you ask.  Well, let’s first look at some history.

   AAnncciieenntt hhiissttoorryy
       There used to be a file _/_e_t_c_/_t_t_y_s in UNIX V6,  that  was  read  by  the
       iinniitt(1)  program  to find out what to do with each terminal line.  Each
       line consisted of three characters.  The first character was either '0'
       or '1', where '0' meant "ignore".  The  second  character  denoted  the
       terminal:  '8' stood for "/dev/tty8".  The third character was an argu‐
       ment to ggeettttyy(8) indicating the sequence of line  speeds  to  try  ('-'
       was: start trying 110 baud).  Thus a typical line was "18-".  A hang on
       some  line  was  solved  by  changing the '1' to a '0', signaling init,
       changing back again, and signaling init again.

       In UNIX V7 the format was changed: here the second  character  was  the
       argument to ggeettttyy(8) indicating the sequence of line speeds to try ('0'
       was:  cycle through 300‐1200‐150‐110 baud; '4' was for the on‐line con‐
       sole DECwriter) while the rest of the line contained the  name  of  the
       tty.  Thus a typical line was "14console".

       Later  systems  have more elaborate syntax.  System V‐like systems have
       _/_e_t_c_/_i_n_i_t_t_a_b instead.

   AAnncciieenntt hhiissttoorryy ((22))
       On the other hand, there is the file _/_e_t_c_/_u_t_m_p listing the people  cur‐
       rently  logged in.  It is maintained by llooggiinn(1).  It has a fixed size,
       and the appropriate index in the file was determined by llooggiinn(1)  using
       the  ttttyysslloott() call to find the number of the line in _/_e_t_c_/_t_t_y_s (count‐
       ing from 1).

   TThhee sseemmaannttiiccss ooff ttttyysslloott
       Thus, the function ttttyysslloott() returns the index of the controlling  ter‐
       minal  of  the calling process in the file _/_e_t_c_/_t_t_y_s, and that is (usu‐
       ally) the same as the index of the entry for the current  user  in  the
       file  _/_e_t_c_/_u_t_m_p.   BSD  still has the _/_e_t_c_/_t_t_y_s file, but System V‐like
       systems do not, and hence cannot refer to it.  Thus,  on  such  systems
       the  documentation says that ttttyysslloott() returns the current user’s index
       in the user accounting data base.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       If successful, this function returns the slot number.  On error  (e.g.,
       if  none of the file descriptors 0, 1, or 2 is associated with a termi‐
       nal that occurs in this data base) it returns 0 on UNIX V6 and  V7  and
       BSD‐like systems, but -1 on System V‐like systems.

